1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,358
It's the end of practice,

2
00:00:01,396 --> 00:00:03,509
and the school track is almost empty.

3
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:05,358
Leo holds up his phone.

4
00:00:05,433 --> 00:00:07,245
His running app says he just hit thirty

5
00:00:07,396 --> 00:00:08,905
kilometers per hour.

6
00:00:08,981 --> 00:00:10,113
Ana doesn't buy it.

7
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:11,547
Phones guess.

8
00:00:11,849 --> 00:00:14,792
So, deal: one run, a measuring wheel,

9
00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:16,981
and a timer that can't be fooled.

10
00:00:17,056 --> 00:00:18,641
If he's really that fast,

11
00:00:18,716 --> 00:00:19,622
she'll admit it.

12
00:00:19,924 --> 00:00:21,132
By the end of this video,

13
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:23,169
you'll check Leo's claim yourself.

14
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,962
Along the way: the units physics runs on,

15
00:00:26,037 --> 00:00:28,226
how to convert between them without guessing,

16
00:00:28,339 --> 00:00:31,018
and how many digits your answer really gets.

17
00:00:31,132 --> 00:00:32,830
This matters more than it looks.

18
00:00:32,905 --> 00:00:34,490
You can pick the right equation,

19
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:36,754
set it up perfectly, and still be off by

20
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:38,943
a factor of a hundred, just because one

21
00:00:39,018 --> 00:00:40,490
value was in centimeters.

22
00:00:40,679 --> 00:00:42,301
And the math won't warn you.

23
00:00:42,339 --> 00:00:44,075
You have to catch it yourself,

24
00:00:44,188 --> 00:00:45,735
and that's what we'll practice.

25
00:00:49,235 --> 00:00:51,877
Every quantity in physics has two parts:

26
00:00:51,915 --> 00:00:53,424
a number and a unit.

27
00:00:53,688 --> 00:00:56,028
If someone says the cart moved three,

28
00:00:56,103 --> 00:00:56,903
three what?

29
00:00:57,084 --> 00:00:57,819
Inches?

30
00:00:57,839 --> 00:00:58,639
Miles?

31
00:00:58,896 --> 00:01:00,783
Three meters is a measurement.

32
00:01:00,858 --> 00:01:03,009
Three on its own is just a number.

33
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:05,990
Scientists agreed on one shared set of units,

34
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:07,877
called SI units.

35
00:01:07,952 --> 00:01:09,839
SI is short for the French name,

36
00:01:09,877 --> 00:01:11,462
the International System.

37
00:01:11,726 --> 00:01:13,726
For this course, you need just three.

38
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:16,330
Meters for length, that's a small m.

39
00:01:16,556 --> 00:01:18,933
Kilograms for mass, kg.

40
00:01:19,122 --> 00:01:21,801
And seconds for time, just s.

41
00:01:22,141 --> 00:01:24,028
Notice something odd about that list?

42
00:01:24,254 --> 00:01:26,745
The kilogram already has a prefix in it,

43
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:29,349
kilo, and it's still the base unit.

44
00:01:29,613 --> 00:01:32,707
So a gram is not the SI unit for mass.

45
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:34,594
If a problem gives you grams,

46
00:01:34,669 --> 00:01:36,632
change them to kilograms first.

47
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:39,198
Every other unit is built out of those three.

48
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:41,990
Speed is a length divided by a time,

49
00:01:42,066 --> 00:01:44,330
so its unit is meters per second.

50
00:01:44,632 --> 00:01:45,915
Here's the payoff.

51
00:01:45,952 --> 00:01:47,349
The equations you'll use,

52
00:01:47,462 --> 00:01:49,084
and constants like the nine point eight

53
00:01:49,235 --> 00:01:50,971
for gravity on the equation sheet,

54
00:01:51,047 --> 00:01:53,160
are all in SI units.

55
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:55,650
Put in meters, kilograms and seconds,

56
00:01:55,726 --> 00:01:58,443
and the answer comes out in SI units too.

57
00:01:58,556 --> 00:02:00,556
Mix in centimeters or grams,

58
00:02:00,632 --> 00:02:02,367
and the answer comes out wrong,

59
00:02:02,556 --> 00:02:04,066
often by a power of ten.

60
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:07,047
A metric prefix in front of a unit multiplies

61
00:02:07,122 --> 00:02:08,707
it by a power of ten.

62
00:02:08,783 --> 00:02:09,990
You need four.

63
00:02:10,066 --> 00:02:12,632
Kilo, small k: a thousand,

64
00:02:12,745 --> 00:02:13,915
ten to the third.

65
00:02:13,952 --> 00:02:16,330
A kilometer is a thousand meters.

66
00:02:16,405 --> 00:02:17,235
Centi?

67
00:02:17,311 --> 00:02:19,952
One hundredth, ten to the negative two.

68
00:02:20,028 --> 00:02:22,858
So a centimeter is point oh one meters.

69
00:02:23,122 --> 00:02:25,462
Milli goes smaller, a thousandth,

70
00:02:25,537 --> 00:02:28,896
so a millisecond is point oh oh one seconds.

71
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:30,518
Now watch the letter m.

72
00:02:30,971 --> 00:02:32,707
ms is milliseconds.

73
00:02:33,084 --> 00:02:34,783
m alone is meters.

74
00:02:34,971 --> 00:02:36,632
Same letter, different job.

75
00:02:37,009 --> 00:02:38,971
And micro, written with the Greek letter

76
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:42,443
mu, is ten to the negative six: a millionth.

77
00:02:43,047 --> 00:02:45,311
The AP equation sheet you get on the exam

78
00:02:45,386 --> 00:02:46,405
lists the rest.

79
00:02:46,669 --> 00:02:48,292
Ana starts with the distance.

80
00:02:48,481 --> 00:02:50,594
The track lines are probably right,

81
00:02:50,707 --> 00:02:53,160
but probably isn't good enough for Ana.

82
00:02:53,273 --> 00:02:55,273
She rolls a measuring wheel down the straight,

83
00:02:55,311 --> 00:02:58,292
and it clicks off one hundred point oh meters.

84
00:02:58,367 --> 00:03:00,405
Then she borrows the coach's laser timing

85
00:03:00,443 --> 00:03:03,273
gates, one at the start and one at the finish.

86
00:03:03,311 --> 00:03:05,235
The clock starts when Leo breaks the first

87
00:03:05,311 --> 00:03:07,801
beam and stops when he breaks the second.

88
00:03:08,002 --> 00:03:10,594
No thumbs on buttons, no guessing.

89
00:03:10,669 --> 00:03:11,575
Your turn.

90
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:14,216
A cart has a mass of two hundred fifty grams,

91
00:03:14,254 --> 00:03:16,707
and a timer reads forty milliseconds.

92
00:03:16,858 --> 00:03:19,198
Write both in SI units, ready to go into

93
00:03:19,235 --> 00:03:20,035
an equation.

94
00:03:26,805 --> 00:03:29,069
Did you get point two five kilograms?

95
00:03:29,145 --> 00:03:31,711
Mass goes in kilograms, so two hundred fifty

96
00:03:31,786 --> 00:03:34,050
grams becomes point two five.

97
00:03:34,164 --> 00:03:35,560
And forty milliseconds?

98
00:03:35,635 --> 00:03:37,447
Point oh four seconds.

99
00:03:37,522 --> 00:03:39,786
The trap is leaving the mass as two hundred

100
00:03:39,937 --> 00:03:40,596
fifty.

101
00:03:40,616 --> 00:03:42,428
It looks like a nice number,

102
00:03:42,503 --> 00:03:44,616
but an equation would read it as two hundred

103
00:03:44,843 --> 00:03:46,428
fifty kilograms.

104
00:03:46,503 --> 00:03:48,390
That's about as much as a motorcycle.

105
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:54,305
So how do you switch from one unit to another

106
00:03:54,456 --> 00:03:55,513
without guessing?

107
00:03:55,626 --> 00:03:58,532
You multiply by a fraction that equals one.

108
00:03:58,607 --> 00:04:00,871
One meter over a hundred centimeters is

109
00:04:01,022 --> 00:04:02,833
one, because the top and the bottom are

110
00:04:02,909 --> 00:04:04,041
the same length.

111
00:04:04,116 --> 00:04:06,381
Multiplying by one can't change how big

112
00:04:06,569 --> 00:04:07,304
something is.

113
00:04:07,324 --> 00:04:09,098
It only changes how it's written.

114
00:04:09,173 --> 00:04:11,286
The whole trick is which way up you put

115
00:04:11,362 --> 00:04:12,267
that fraction.

116
00:04:12,343 --> 00:04:14,104
Put the unit you want on top,

117
00:04:14,129 --> 00:04:15,890
and the unit you want to get rid of on the

118
00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:16,650
bottom.

119
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:19,336
A pencil is eighteen centimeters long.

120
00:04:19,463 --> 00:04:22,003
Times one meter over a hundred centimeters.

121
00:04:22,079 --> 00:04:24,305
Centimeters on top cancel centimeters on

122
00:04:24,343 --> 00:04:26,053
the bottom, just like numbers,

123
00:04:26,104 --> 00:04:28,607
and you're left with point one eight meters.

124
00:04:28,645 --> 00:04:30,355
Then check that it makes sense.

125
00:04:30,406 --> 00:04:32,381
A meter is bigger than a centimeter,

126
00:04:32,418 --> 00:04:33,878
so you need fewer of them,

127
00:04:33,902 --> 00:04:35,550
and the number got smaller.

128
00:04:35,588 --> 00:04:37,475
If it had gotten bigger, your fraction was

129
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:38,569
upside down.

130
00:04:38,607 --> 00:04:41,815
That one check catches most conversion mistakes.

131
00:04:41,852 --> 00:04:43,626
Speeds take two fractions,

132
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:44,947
one for each unit.

133
00:04:45,022 --> 00:04:47,211
Say a car drives at seventy two kilometers

134
00:04:47,286 --> 00:04:48,086
per hour.

135
00:04:48,116 --> 00:04:50,708
Times a thousand meters over one kilometer,

136
00:04:50,733 --> 00:04:52,230
and the kilometers are gone.

137
00:04:52,267 --> 00:04:53,588
Now the hours.

138
00:04:53,626 --> 00:04:56,116
Times one hour over three thousand six hundred

139
00:04:56,192 --> 00:04:56,992
seconds.

140
00:04:57,022 --> 00:04:59,060
That leaves seventy two thousand meters

141
00:04:59,211 --> 00:05:01,374
over three thousand six hundred seconds,

142
00:05:01,425 --> 00:05:03,324
which is twenty meters per second.

143
00:05:03,362 --> 00:05:05,739
Look at what those two fractions do together:

144
00:05:05,777 --> 00:05:08,468
a thousand over three thousand six hundred.

145
00:05:08,519 --> 00:05:10,456
That's the same as dividing by three point

146
00:05:10,796 --> 00:05:11,191
six.

147
00:05:11,211 --> 00:05:14,003
So kilometers per hour to meters per second,

148
00:05:14,154 --> 00:05:15,777
divide by three point six.

149
00:05:15,815 --> 00:05:17,500
Going back, multiply.

150
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:19,437
And if you ever forget which way the shortcut

151
00:05:19,513 --> 00:05:20,247
goes...

152
00:05:20,267 --> 00:05:22,116
just write the fractions out again.

153
00:05:22,577 --> 00:05:24,720
Now, here's a trap that catches a lot of

154
00:05:24,796 --> 00:05:25,596
people.

155
00:05:25,626 --> 00:05:26,708
Areas!

156
00:05:26,808 --> 00:05:29,362
Picture a square, one meter on each side.

157
00:05:29,399 --> 00:05:31,475
How many square centimeters is that?

158
00:05:31,513 --> 00:05:33,513
Each side is a hundred centimeters,

159
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:35,928
so the area is a hundred times a hundred,

160
00:05:35,966 --> 00:05:37,966
ten thousand square centimeters,

161
00:05:38,003 --> 00:05:39,098
not a hundred.

162
00:05:39,135 --> 00:05:41,550
For an area, you use the conversion fraction

163
00:05:41,626 --> 00:05:43,966
twice, once for each length.

164
00:05:44,041 --> 00:05:46,343
Leo backs way up for a running start,

165
00:05:46,381 --> 00:05:48,456
so the gates catch him at top speed,

166
00:05:48,494 --> 00:05:50,192
not while he's still speeding up.

167
00:05:50,230 --> 00:05:51,412
And he's off!

168
00:05:51,463 --> 00:05:53,928
He breaks the first beam already flying...

169
00:05:54,003 --> 00:05:55,475
and then the second one.

170
00:05:55,513 --> 00:05:57,815
The display shows thousandths of a second:

171
00:05:57,852 --> 00:06:00,305
twelve point four three one seconds.

172
00:06:00,343 --> 00:06:01,815
So what was his speed?

173
00:06:01,852 --> 00:06:03,399
Distance over time.

174
00:06:03,437 --> 00:06:05,550
Ana divides one hundred point oh meters

175
00:06:05,626 --> 00:06:08,154
by twelve point four three one seconds.

176
00:06:08,192 --> 00:06:10,720
Eight point oh four four meters per second...

177
00:06:10,758 --> 00:06:12,984
and that's his average between the gates.

178
00:06:13,022 --> 00:06:15,173
But the app said kilometers per hour,

179
00:06:15,211 --> 00:06:16,381
so she converts.

180
00:06:16,418 --> 00:06:17,852
Times three point six.

181
00:06:17,928 --> 00:06:19,815
That gives about twenty eight point nine

182
00:06:19,966 --> 00:06:21,550
six kilometers per hour.

183
00:06:21,777 --> 00:06:22,758
So close!

184
00:06:23,211 --> 00:06:26,343
But it's not thirty, and Leo is not happy.

185
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:27,664
He wants another run.

186
00:06:28,116 --> 00:06:30,381
And Ana notices her calculator showed a

187
00:06:30,532 --> 00:06:32,720
lot more digits than she wrote down.

188
00:06:33,022 --> 00:06:33,822
Your turn.

189
00:06:34,003 --> 00:06:36,418
A car on the highway is going ninety kilometers

190
00:06:36,494 --> 00:06:37,399
per hour.

191
00:06:37,475 --> 00:06:39,324
What's that in meters per second?

192
00:06:45,988 --> 00:06:47,573
Ninety kilometers per hour,

193
00:06:47,649 --> 00:06:50,215
times a thousand meters over one kilometer,

194
00:06:50,403 --> 00:06:52,781
times one hour over three thousand six hundred

195
00:06:52,932 --> 00:06:53,837
seconds.

196
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:56,101
That's twenty five meters per second.

197
00:06:56,215 --> 00:06:59,271
Or just ninety divided by three point six.

198
00:06:59,347 --> 00:07:01,158
If you got three hundred twenty four,

199
00:07:01,233 --> 00:07:03,233
you multiplied by three point six.

200
00:07:03,498 --> 00:07:06,215
Check the size: times a thousand makes it

201
00:07:06,366 --> 00:07:08,932
bigger, divided by three thousand six hundred

202
00:07:09,007 --> 00:07:11,347
makes it smaller, and the three thousand

203
00:07:11,422 --> 00:07:12,705
six hundred wins.

204
00:07:12,781 --> 00:07:15,347
So the answer has to be smaller than ninety.

205
00:07:18,847 --> 00:07:21,262
First, a quick tool for writing very big

206
00:07:21,413 --> 00:07:24,771
and very small numbers: scientific notation.

207
00:07:25,035 --> 00:07:27,149
It's a number from one up to just under

208
00:07:27,337 --> 00:07:29,111
ten, times a power of ten,

209
00:07:29,186 --> 00:07:31,375
so everyone writes the same number the same

210
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:32,250
way.

211
00:07:32,356 --> 00:07:34,318
Take a camera shutter open for point oh

212
00:07:34,469 --> 00:07:35,866
oh four oh seconds.

213
00:07:36,054 --> 00:07:37,715
Slide the decimal point right,

214
00:07:37,790 --> 00:07:41,186
one, two, three, until the four is out front.

215
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,601
Three slides right, so it's four point oh

216
00:07:43,752 --> 00:07:45,601
times ten to the negative three.

217
00:07:45,866 --> 00:07:47,903
A run that lasts seven thousand two hundred

218
00:07:48,054 --> 00:07:50,620
seconds slides three places left: seven

219
00:07:50,771 --> 00:07:52,658
point two times ten to the third.

220
00:07:53,111 --> 00:07:54,922
Small number, negative power.

221
00:07:55,224 --> 00:07:57,564
That means small, not negative.

222
00:07:57,790 --> 00:08:00,281
And on your calculator, E just means times

223
00:08:00,356 --> 00:08:01,413
ten to the.

224
00:08:01,866 --> 00:08:04,356
Now, how many digits should an answer have?

225
00:08:04,583 --> 00:08:06,847
A measurement is only known so well.

226
00:08:07,073 --> 00:08:08,960
Read a meterstick with centimeter marks,

227
00:08:09,035 --> 00:08:11,526
and you can see it's past forty five centimeters.

228
00:08:11,752 --> 00:08:14,243
Then you estimate between the marks: point

229
00:08:14,469 --> 00:08:16,130
four five two meters.

230
00:08:16,205 --> 00:08:18,545
That last two is your best guess.

231
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:20,733
Your calculator might say point four five

232
00:08:20,847 --> 00:08:21,866
two three seven...

233
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:24,130
and on and on, but nobody measured those

234
00:08:24,281 --> 00:08:25,413
extra digits.

235
00:08:25,526 --> 00:08:27,828
Significant figures are the digits you actually

236
00:08:27,903 --> 00:08:28,638
measured.

237
00:08:28,658 --> 00:08:30,998
That includes your last best guess.

238
00:08:31,073 --> 00:08:32,356
Here's how you count them.

239
00:08:32,809 --> 00:08:34,771
Nonzero digits always count,

240
00:08:34,847 --> 00:08:37,035
and so do zeros sandwiched between them.

241
00:08:37,111 --> 00:08:39,526
Eight point oh six one has four.

242
00:08:39,601 --> 00:08:40,507
Quick test.

243
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:42,167
Point oh four five oh.

244
00:08:42,205 --> 00:08:43,149
How many?

245
00:08:43,224 --> 00:08:43,405
...

246
00:08:43,425 --> 00:08:44,034
Three.

247
00:08:44,054 --> 00:08:45,752
The zeros in front are just holding the

248
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:46,696
decimal point.

249
00:08:46,922 --> 00:08:48,167
But that zero at the end?

250
00:08:48,356 --> 00:08:50,620
It counts, because there's a decimal point.

251
00:08:51,073 --> 00:08:53,262
Same with fifteen point oh: three.

252
00:08:53,715 --> 00:08:55,828
Without a decimal point, like seven thousand

253
00:08:55,903 --> 00:08:58,394
two hundred, the end zeros are unclear.

254
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:00,884
So if you mean two significant figures,

255
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,866
write seven point two times ten to the third.

256
00:09:04,356 --> 00:09:05,941
When you multiply or divide,

257
00:09:05,979 --> 00:09:08,054
the answer keeps as many significant figures

258
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:09,866
as the value with the fewest,

259
00:09:09,979 --> 00:09:11,903
because your answer is only as good as your

260
00:09:11,941 --> 00:09:13,413
sloppiest measurement.

261
00:09:13,488 --> 00:09:15,450
Two hundred point oh meters over twenty

262
00:09:15,496 --> 00:09:17,111
four point eight seconds.

263
00:09:17,186 --> 00:09:19,450
The distance has four significant figures,

264
00:09:19,488 --> 00:09:21,262
but the time has only three.

265
00:09:21,337 --> 00:09:23,790
So the answer gets three: eight point oh

266
00:09:23,903 --> 00:09:26,092
six meters per second.

267
00:09:26,167 --> 00:09:28,356
Adding and subtracting work differently.

268
00:09:28,507 --> 00:09:30,545
There, you look at decimal places.

269
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:32,658
Eight point seven meters plus two point

270
00:09:32,771 --> 00:09:33,941
four three meters.

271
00:09:34,016 --> 00:09:36,733
The calculator says eleven point one three.

272
00:09:36,809 --> 00:09:38,620
But nobody knows the hundredths place of

273
00:09:38,696 --> 00:09:40,884
eight point seven, so the sum can't know

274
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:42,243
its hundredths either.

275
00:09:42,318 --> 00:09:44,960
The answer stops at the tenths place: eleven

276
00:09:45,111 --> 00:09:46,620
point one meters.

277
00:09:47,149 --> 00:09:48,318
Two last rules.

278
00:09:48,507 --> 00:09:50,620
Conversion factors like a thousand meters

279
00:09:50,696 --> 00:09:52,809
per kilometer, or three point six,

280
00:09:52,884 --> 00:09:55,299
come from definitions, so they're exact.

281
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:57,111
They never limit your digits.

282
00:09:57,413 --> 00:09:59,601
And round once, at the very end.

283
00:09:59,828 --> 00:10:01,715
Keep the extra digits in your calculator

284
00:10:01,941 --> 00:10:04,130
between steps, because rounding early can

285
00:10:04,205 --> 00:10:05,337
change the answer.

286
00:10:05,639 --> 00:10:07,564
Back on the track, Ana counts.

287
00:10:07,752 --> 00:10:10,167
One hundred point oh meters has four significant

288
00:10:10,243 --> 00:10:12,356
figures, and twelve point four three one

289
00:10:12,432 --> 00:10:13,828
seconds has five.

290
00:10:14,016 --> 00:10:16,658
The fewest wins, so Leo's first run was

291
00:10:16,733 --> 00:10:19,299
eight point oh four four meters per second,

292
00:10:19,375 --> 00:10:20,175
four figures.

293
00:10:20,507 --> 00:10:21,866
Meanwhile, where's Leo?

294
00:10:22,243 --> 00:10:23,325
Already back at the start,

295
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:24,149
of course!

296
00:10:24,356 --> 00:10:25,156
Second run.

297
00:10:25,262 --> 00:10:27,073
He flies through the beams...

298
00:10:27,149 --> 00:10:29,413
and this time the timer says eleven point

299
00:10:29,488 --> 00:10:30,998
nine nine eight seconds.

300
00:10:31,224 --> 00:10:33,035
Now watch what early rounding would do.

301
00:10:33,413 --> 00:10:35,677
Say Ana gets impatient and rounds the speed

302
00:10:35,752 --> 00:10:38,167
to three digits, too early: eight point

303
00:10:38,243 --> 00:10:39,224
three three.

304
00:10:39,375 --> 00:10:41,262
Times three point six gives twenty nine

305
00:10:41,413 --> 00:10:42,620
point nine nine.

306
00:10:42,696 --> 00:10:44,281
Just under, again!

307
00:10:44,507 --> 00:10:46,016
But what if she does it right?

308
00:10:46,167 --> 00:10:48,054
Keep every digit on the calculator,

309
00:10:48,092 --> 00:10:51,149
and round only once, at the very end: thirty

310
00:10:51,299 --> 00:10:53,375
point oh one kilometers per hour.

311
00:10:53,639 --> 00:10:56,130
By the numbers, he just made it.

312
00:10:56,205 --> 00:10:57,866
That last digit is a coin flip,

313
00:10:57,941 --> 00:10:59,677
so call it a photo finish...

314
00:10:59,715 --> 00:11:01,639
but you'd never even see it if you rounded

315
00:11:01,828 --> 00:11:02,628
early.

316
00:11:02,696 --> 00:11:04,507
Your turn, two quick ones.

317
00:11:04,696 --> 00:11:06,696
First, how many significant figures are

318
00:11:06,771 --> 00:11:09,564
in point oh oh seven two oh meters?

319
00:11:09,752 --> 00:11:12,205
Second, add twelve point oh meters and three

320
00:11:12,356 --> 00:11:15,111
point two seven meters, and report it properly.

321
00:11:21,775 --> 00:11:24,039
The first one has three: the seven,

322
00:11:24,077 --> 00:11:25,926
the two, and the zero at the end.

323
00:11:26,077 --> 00:11:28,001
The zeros in front don't count.

324
00:11:28,190 --> 00:11:30,379
For the sum, the calculator gives fifteen

325
00:11:30,530 --> 00:11:31,737
point two seven.

326
00:11:31,813 --> 00:11:33,775
Twelve point oh only goes to the tenths

327
00:11:33,850 --> 00:11:36,567
place, so round there: fifteen point three

328
00:11:36,718 --> 00:11:37,518
meters.

329
00:11:37,674 --> 00:11:39,737
The trap is counting significant figures

330
00:11:39,888 --> 00:11:41,700
here, the way you would when multiplying.

331
00:11:42,001 --> 00:11:44,190
For adding, it's decimal places.

332
00:11:47,690 --> 00:11:50,256
So here's the whole video in four checks

333
00:11:50,294 --> 00:11:52,520
you can use on every calculation.

334
00:11:52,822 --> 00:11:55,690
One: before anything goes into an equation,

335
00:11:55,841 --> 00:11:58,671
convert it to meters, kilograms and seconds.

336
00:11:58,935 --> 00:12:01,501
Two: carry the units through each step.

337
00:12:01,652 --> 00:12:03,464
Do they cancel to the unit you expect?

338
00:12:03,992 --> 00:12:06,256
Three: does the size make sense?

339
00:12:06,483 --> 00:12:08,898
Nobody walks at thirty meters per second!

340
00:12:09,275 --> 00:12:10,181
And four...

341
00:12:10,332 --> 00:12:12,520
round once, at the end, to the significant

342
00:12:12,671 --> 00:12:14,256
figures your data support.

343
00:12:17,756 --> 00:12:19,869
Of course, Leo wants proof for the team

344
00:12:19,945 --> 00:12:20,850
group chat.

345
00:12:20,964 --> 00:12:23,266
So Ana snaps a photo of the timer screen

346
00:12:23,379 --> 00:12:25,303
and sends it with one line...

347
00:12:25,341 --> 00:12:27,039
thirty point oh one!

348
00:12:27,115 --> 00:12:28,850
The notes and practice for this topic are

349
00:12:29,039 --> 00:12:31,115
free on levlprep.com.

350
00:12:31,228 --> 00:12:34,133
Next time: Leo ran a hundred meters.

351
00:12:34,284 --> 00:12:36,888
But was that a distance or a displacement?

352
00:12:37,152 --> 00:12:39,567
In physics, those aren't the same thing.

353
00:12:39,643 --> 00:12:41,605
That's scalars and vectors.

